"Introducing Mistral Large 4 Mistral AI has announced the public preview of 'Mistral Large 4,' a natively multimodal open-weight model with 1 trillion parameters. Mistral Large 4 (ML4) is a natively multimodal model with 49 billion active parameters out of 1 trillion total parameters, delivering exceptional performance across coding, agent workflows, and multimodal understanding. Trained from scratch using 3,800 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GPUs in its proprietary European data centers, its open weights are scheduled for official release at the end of this month. It delivers state-of-the-art open-model performance in enterprise environments such as cybersecurity, finance, and legal sectors, enabling organizations to deploy and operate autonomously according to their own policies. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI startup Mistral has established a new hub in Munich, Germany—Europe's largest economy—and launched a full-scale push into the manufacturing and industrial AI markets. Mistral has officially opened a new hub in Munich, Germany, deploying research teams focused on industrial AI and Physics AI alongside field deployment engineers. The company is collaborating closely with BMW on crash simulation and engineering AI, and with Siemens Energy on developing industrial AI applications. Aiming to secure European AI sovereignty, Mistral plans to build one gigawatt (GW) of compute capacity across Europe by 2030 and support independent infrastructure deployments based on open-weight architectures. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral and Mozilla are partnering to introduce Mistral AI models to Firefox's AI browsing assistant, 'Firefox Smart Window,' providing privacy-focused, multilingual browsing capabilities. Mozilla's AI browsing assistant, 'Firefox Smart Window (beta),' is powered by Mistral models, supporting features such as synthesizing complex searches and tab-based information retrieval. It is initially available to users in France and North America, with support expected to expand to the United Kingdom and Germany later this year. It provides a localized AI experience by applying multilingual models fine-tuned to local languages, dialects, and cultural contexts. User privacy is strongly protected as conversations are not saved on Mozilla's servers by default, and Mistral has agreed to a zero data retention policy. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Cloudera and Mistral AI have formed a partnership to provide Sovereign AI solutions that enable enterprises to build customized intelligence while maintaining data sovereignty. Mistral's AI models are integrated with Cloudera's hybrid data platform, allowing inference to run under full control across on-premises, public/private cloud, and air-gapped environments. It empowers enterprises to train proprietary models using their large-scale proprietary data within controlled environments, retaining complete ownership of their data and intelligence. It targets demand from enterprises in regulated industries such as finance, manufacturing, and telecommunications seeking to move beyond renting generic AI to building specialized AI under their own control. Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. Covers a case study where Mistral AI agents were successfully utilized to modernize a complex, 40,000-line Fortran 77 legacy codebase—which lacked tests and documentation—into modern C++ for a European energy company. Went beyond simple syntax translation to perform architectural refactoring from a procedural language to object-oriented C++ and integrate the PETSc framework. Built an equivalence parity harness for numerical verification to validate the accuracy of intermediate and final computation results. Ensured code quality by documenting the codebase with AI agents prior to migration and combining a structured workflow with human review. OpenAI’s Alexander Embiricos is coming to TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 — days after the launch of Dots News that OpenAI's Alexander Embiricos will speak on the AI Stage at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 shortly after the launch of Dots. Alexander Embiricos from OpenAI is scheduled to appear on the AI Stage at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026. This appearance comes just days after the launch of OpenAI's new project, Dots. Registration for TechCrunch Disrupt event passes and discount promotions are currently underway. Get hands-on: The full lineup of interactive roundtables at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 Introduces the full schedule of major interactive roundtables and registration discounts available at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026. The roundtable lineup featuring major companies and investors including Nvidia, Chime, Obvious Ventures, and Anthropic has been unveiled. You can save up to $100 on pass purchases by registering early. A 50% discount is offered when purchasing a second pass. Google’s new SynthID website can identify AI-generated media Google has officially launched the SynthID website, allowing anyone to identify whether media such as images, videos, and audio were generated by AI. The SynthID detection tool, which was restricted to select journalists and researchers at last year's Google I/O, has now been fully opened to the general public. It supports image, video, and audio files across various formats, and in addition to Google's proprietary models (Nano Banana, Veo, Lyria, etc.) and tools, companies such as OpenAI, Nvidia, and Kakao also support SynthID. Google has integrated this verification feature into the Gemini app and Chrome, and it is currently processing 1 million verification requests per day. Google invests millions in Mark Zuckerberg’s efforts to create a ‘virtual cell’ Google DeepMind, Meta, and Isomorphic Labs have jointly invested $300 million into Biohub with the goal of building a virtual cell for biological simulation. Google DeepMind, Meta, and Isomorphic Labs have jointly invested $300 million into Biohub, a non-profit biomedical research institution founded by Mark Zuckerberg and Priscilla Chan. This investment is part of a $1.8 billion initiative to build AI datasets that enable biological inquiries and predictions digitally, helping disease prevention and management. The U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) will contribute more than $500 million over the next five years, and the National Institutes of Health (NIH) will also provide datasets based on more than $500 million in prior federal investments. AI could upend food delivery An examination of the shifts in the food delivery industry and the crisis facing platform business models, explored through the emergence of AI agent-based startup Bites and the ensuing conflict with DoorDash. As AI agents handle orders and tasks on behalf of users, traditional delivery apps and websites face the risk of losing direct touchpoints with consumers as well as control over upselling. DoorDash sent warning letters to its partner restaurants, keeping a wary eye on Bites, a 10-person emerging AI delivery startup, alleging unauthorized listing of restaurants in the Bay Area. Bites is pursuing a differentiation strategy that lowers the price burden on both restaurants and consumers by offering a flat fee of $1 per order instead of DoorDash's high commission fees of 15% to 30%. ChatGPT for Teens is an ‘unacceptable risk,’ says Common Sense Media The nonprofit organization Common Sense Media assessed that OpenAI's ChatGPT for teens poses an unacceptable level of risk due to inadequate safety measures. Common Sense Media pointed out that ChatGPT for teens falls short of promised protections, failing to adequately alert parents during crisis situations and still completing homework on behalf of students. Director Tom Siegel stated that a teen could talk about self-harm for an hour without their parents receiving an alert, arguing that it should remain restricted to adults until improvements are made. OpenAI countered that the test results were inaccurate because the evaluation was conducted before parental control features were fully activated; however, Common Sense Media reaffirmed the validity of their test results, noting that no notifications were received even on accounts linked for an extended period. ML Engineer, AI Engineer, or LLM Engineer: Which Role Actually Builds What in 2026? An article comparing and analyzing the actual responsibilities and development deliverables of ML Engineer, AI Engineer, and LLM Engineer roles, which are often used interchangeably in job postings. A Machine Learning Engineer (ML Engineer) is responsible for collecting and cleaning data, training models directly, and deploying and operating them in large-scale production environments. An AI Engineer focuses on leveraging pre-trained model APIs to integrate and implement real-world service products, such as customer support tools, internal search features, and agents. An LLM Engineer specializes in Large Language Models, going beyond API integration to deeply perform fine-tuning of pre-trained model weights using techniques like LoRA. I Tested 5 AI Coding Assistants for a Month: Here’s What I Actually Found An article analyzing the differences in philosophy and real-world performance among five major AI coding assistants, tested and compared over a month in actual development environments. Each AI coding tool pursues a different direction, such as a complete editor overhaul (Cursor), enhancing existing environments (GitHub Copilot), terminal-based collaboration (Claude Code), autonomous execution (Windsurf), and browser-based one-stop development (Replit Agent). While Cursor excels in multi-file context awareness and refactoring, it shows limitations in ambiguous tasks within large codebases, such as modifying code outside the intended scope. Going beyond traditional inline code autocomplete, the autonomy of agent mode—capable of editing files and executing terminal commands—is emerging as a key differentiator in practical workflows. GAMEGO: Training Game-Dev Agents with Synthetic Trajectories Anchored in Real-World Assets This study introduces GAMEGO, a framework that trains game development agents using synthetic trajectory data anchored in real-world industrial assets to enable the creation of high-quality games from brief input prompts. Systematically transforms simple game seed inputs into detailed product requirements documents (PRDs) based on industry standards, enabling design exploration while maintaining constraints through dynamic compression techniques Constructs GameGoData, consisting of 55,060 development trajectories spanning 2D, 2.5D, and 3D games, along with GameGoBench, a benchmark comprising 124 diverse game queries Demonstrates that the GameGoCoder model trained on this data outperforms existing baseline models and achieves game development performance comparable to state-of-the-art frontier models FluidPD: In-Place Elasticity for SLO-Aware Prefill-Decode Disaggregated LLM Serving This research introduces FluidPD, an in-place elasticity system that resolves dynamic imbalances between prefill and decode phases in real time to improve SLO attainment rates in LLM serving environments. Existing prefill-decode disaggregation (P/D disaggregation) architectures have limitations where fixed worker ratios lead to latency SLO violations during short-term bursts or sustained demand shifts, even when idle resources exist. FluidPD combines FluidToken, which offloads partial prefill computations when decode workers are idle, with FluidRole, a mechanism that switches the roles of running workers without reloading models. By proactively detecting resource pressure using lightweight pressure indicators, it flexibly reallocates resources in place without requiring additional GPU provisioning. Anchor Divergence for Semantic Geometry in Contrastive Learning This paper proposes Anchor Divergences to overcome the limitations of fixed cosine similarity in contrastive representation spaces and to define context-dependent semantic geometry. It highlights that, unlike the fixed geometric structure based on conventional cosine similarity, semantic similarity depends on context. By leveraging the interplay among contrastive learning, exponential families, and information geometry, it establishes a correspondence between anchor probability distributions and Bregman geometry. Through retrieval experiments, it demonstrates that modeling anchor distributions enables the efficient definition of context-specific geometry even within a fixed representation space. RadOnc-Agent: An LLM-Orchestrated Framework for AI Workflows Across the Radiotherapy Care Pathway This study presents RadOnc-Agent, an agent framework that integrates and orchestrates previously fragmented AI capabilities across the entire radiotherapy care pathway using a large language model (LLM) controller. Developed the RadOnc-Agent framework, which formalizes the radiotherapy process into four clinical stages and provides 26 callable functions accessible via a conversational interface. Achieved a 98.79% selection accuracy for single-function requests, a 96.50% completion rate in synthetic cross-stage workflows, and a 96.67% completion rate in workflows based on 60 de-identified patient records. Demonstrated the technical feasibility of LLM-based orchestration by confirming that longitudinal context preservation and schema validation are critical for accurate backend routing and workflow completion. Principles that Guide, Actions that Inform: Agent Evolution via Knowledge Abstraction This research introduces SAGA, a framework that supports the continuous evolution of LLM agents by progressively abstracting interaction experiences into generalizable principles. Existing external memory-based agents have limitations in generalization because experiential knowledge remains tied to specific tasks and contexts. The SAGA framework progressively abstracts agent interaction trajectories into principles equipped with episodic descriptions, reusable procedures, and explicit application conditions. It concretizes extracted principles into task-specific instructions and refines actions through feedback, demonstrating improved performance in ScienceWorld and ALFWorld experiments. TEMPEST: Temporal Embeddings for Scalable Driver Identification via Angular Margin Learning A study proposing TEMPEST, a temporal convolutional neural network embedding model based on additive angular margin learning for scalable driver identification. Applies ArcFace loss to overcome the limitations of conventional triplet-loss methods, inducing global class separation in a normalized angular space. Maps 60 seconds of multimodal driving data into 96-dimensional embeddings, supporting dynamic driver enrollment without the need for retraining or classifier fine-tuning. Demonstrates high scalability and cross-session generalization performance with a lightweight model of 720K parameters and 2.80 MB in size, achieving 91.71% Rank-1 accuracy on a 45-driver dataset. When Does External Guidance Help LLM Reasoning? A Bias-Variance Theory of Guidance-Augmented GRPO This paper presents a bias-variance theoretical framework and an optimal weighting rule for reinforcement learning (GRPO) techniques combined with external guidance to enhance the reasoning capabilities of large language models. Proposed GA-GRPO (Guidance-Augmented GRPO), a unified framework encompassing existing methodologies that incorporate external guidance (such as expert trajectories and self-explanations). Proved that GA-GRPO achieves an O(1/sqrt(T)) convergence rate, and derived a closed-form optimal weighting rule (lambda-star) to offset bias along with a minimax lower bound. Experimental results based on Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Base demonstrated comparable or superior performance while reducing computation time (GPU hours) by 31% compared to existing methods. Neutrosophic Ensemble Classification for Uncertainty-Aware Bearing Fault Detection: Evidence from Laboratory and Variable-Speed Industrial Benchmarks This study applies a neutrosophic uncertainty decomposition technique to ensemble models to separate erroneous overconfidence from ambiguous predictions in bearing fault diagnosis, evaluating the approach across laboratory and industrial benchmark settings. To overcome the limitations of confidence scores in conventional machine learning, uncertainty was measured by decomposing an ensemble of Random Forest, XGBoost, and Logistic Regression into four neutrosophic metrics: T-hat, F-hat, predictive entropy I1-hat, and decision disagreement I2-hat. On the CWRU benchmark, after correcting mapping errors, it achieved 100.00% accuracy across three load conditions; however, on the JNU benchmark (excluding 1000 rpm), accuracy plummeted to 40.64%, with standalone Logistic Regression (57.91%) showing superior generalization compared to tree-based ensembles. Fusing time-domain and frequency-domain models and measuring Jensen-Shannon divergence outperformed standalone entropy (AURC 0.29 vs. 0.36) and accurately captured distribution shifts. Comparative review of hybrid forecasting models for short-term prediction of building thermal load This paper presents a comparative analysis of the performance of various hybrid artificial intelligence models for short-term prediction of building thermal loads. Analyzed hybrid techniques reported in existing literature to qualitatively evaluate input/output characteristics, key strengths and weaknesses, parameter identification methods, and more. Comparatively evaluated the performance of 13 hybrid forecasting models based on historical heat demand and weather forecast datasets from Scottish households. Evaluation results showed that the EMD-LSTM-Markov model predicted day-ahead power patterns for space heating and domestic hot water demand with the highest accuracy, though it exhibited limitations in somewhat underestimating sharp power spikes. Event-Driven ML Pipeline Orchestration for Manufacturing: An AWS Industry Experience Shares three years of industrial operational experience orchestrating machine learning pipelines efficiently using event-driven AWS cloud infrastructure in automotive manufacturing processes. Coordinated the training of physics prediction models and reinforcement learning control policy models by combining Amazon ECS, EC2 GPU capacity providers, SQS, and an admission control Lambda dispatcher for concurrency limiting Achieved 72-78% cost savings compared to always-on GPU infrastructure across more than 40,000 actual production training jobs operated Proved through discrete-event simulation that admission control is essential to prevent the 65% job loss that occurs with naive dispatch, and open-sourced the simulator and Terraform module skeleton Zero-Shot Visualization: Exploring Text Corpora with User-Prompted Axes This paper proposes the Zero-Shot Visualization (ZSV) methodology and design guidelines, which visualize document corpora by mapping them onto axes defined in natural language by users. It introduces the Zero-Shot Visualization (ZSV) task, which allows users to visually explore text corpora by mapping documents onto conceptual axes specified in natural language. After evaluating various scoring techniques such as embedding similarity, direct semantic judgment, and conditional likelihood estimation, next-token probability-based scoring demonstrated the best practical balance in terms of semantic fidelity and computational cost. It presents specific design considerations and guidelines for practical corpus exploration, including the necessity of combining binary relevance filtering with staged axes, as well as addressing compound sentiment bias found in off-topic documents. Tree Navigation Without LLM Summaries: A Matched-Cost Study of Hierarchical Retrieval for Long-Document QA This study proposes the NavTree retriever, which performs efficient retrieval in long-document question answering (QA) using only deterministic balanced tree navigation without generating LLM summaries. The study identifies that the benefits gained from summary trees in the conventional RAPTOR approach stem from the tree-based navigation structure itself rather than the generated summary content. The authors propose NavTree, which incurs zero LLM call costs during indexing and ultimately delivers only leaf chunks to reader models via hybrid navigation combining lexical and dense retrieval. Under matched-cost evaluation and long-document multi-hop QA settings, NavTree demonstrated the highest performance among evaluated hierarchical retrievers, outperforming BM25 and RAPTOR variants that utilize summary trees. Component and Dimension Sparsity in Transformer Refusal Mechanisms This research reveals that refusal mechanisms in transformer language models are not diffusely distributed across the entire model, but are instead sparsely concentrated in specific components and dimensions. By analyzing refusal steering across four open-weight models, the study identified a sparse subset of attention and MLP components capable of reproducing the full effect. Refusal directions are concentrated in 28--48% of the top components, maintaining 88--101% of the overall steering effect. Within these mechanisms, effects are also concentrated in approximately 50% of the residual stream dimensions, preserving 85--98% of baseline performance and demonstrating that sparsity operates at both the component and dimension levels. Stabilizing language models under continual learning via condition-anchored distillation This study investigates Condition-Anchored Generative Distillation (CAGD), a technique that leverages a small number of prior prompts and distillation from a frozen prior model to prevent performance degradation of language models in continual learning environments. In continual learning scenarios where retaining all past data is difficult, CAGD was proposed to match predictive distributions by utilizing a small number of previous prompts and a frozen previous model. Unlike conventional replay methods, it decouples three roles—selecting behaviors to protect (conditions), exploring relevant states (teacher model generations), and specifying tolerances for prediction changes (soft targets)—and was applied to both autoregressive and masked diffusion models. In an adaptation evaluation of a 219M masked diffusion model across four tasks, it substantially reduced the final loss from 2.927 to 1.114, confirming consistent performance preservation across recent facts and natural language instruction following for SMDM and Qwen3 models. Verdicts Without Annotated Evidence: Rejection Sampling or Label-Only Post-Training for Evidence Recovery? A study on improving evidence recovery performance in small language models through post-training and rejection sampling using only final verdict labels without human evidence annotations. Because the correlation between verdict match rate and actual human evidence span match rate is low, verdict accuracy alone is insufficient to evaluate evidence citation quality. The model post-trained with only verdict labels recorded an accuracy of 0.896 and a span F1 of 0.564, while the rejection sampling method achieved an accuracy of 0.797 and a span F1 of 0.556. Both methods significantly improved the verbatim citation rate from 0.597 before training to 0.729 and 0.701, respectively, without human evidence annotations. One Model Family, Two Gold-Level Results: Fine-Tuning Nemotron for IOI and IMO This research presents NVIDIA's achievement of gold-medal-level performance in both the International Olympiad in Informatics (IOI) and the International Mathematical Olympiad (IMO) by fine-tuning a single base model, Nemotron 3. By utilizing Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), Reinforcement Learning (RL), and a feedback-based reasoning loop, Nemotron 3 was specialized into expert models for competitive programming and mathematical proof without the need for a new foundational model. In competitive programming, applying an iterative verification and refinement strategy (GenCorrect) enabled Nemotron-3-Nano-CC to score 468 points and Ultra-CC to score 535.4 out of 600 based on IOI 2026 criteria, surpassing the gold medal threshold. In Olympiad mathematics, by training beyond simple answer generation to include proof generation, verification, and critique response using 15,818 problems and a curated dataset of 414,890 examples, the model achieved gold-medal-level performance evaluated by official IMO graders. Show GN: Where Is My Cross-Border Package Right Now? Introducing 'Baboship,' an open-source service that enables users to track the location of cross-border purchase packages for free and securely using real logistics data. Unlike conventional simple LLM prediction methods, it precisely tracks package locations based on real data from cities, EMS, airports, and maritime shipping. It protects personal privacy by processing data on a one-time basis within the browser without requiring users to input their residential address. Released as open source, anyone can download or modify the code, helping eliminate the cost of paid prediction services. Strands Decider 2B - Compact Open-Source Decision-Making Model Strands Decider 2B, a 2-billion-parameter open-source decision-making model designed to quickly and efficiently handle agentic decision-making—such as tool selection and model routing—instead of text generation, has been released. Based on Qwen3.5-2B, it replaces the text generation head with an option-evaluating pointer head, deriving fast decisions and confidence scores in a single forward pass. It is well-suited for hybrid architectures that reduce cost and latency by offloading repetitive agent judgment tasks, such as argument validation prior to tool execution and deciding whether to request user reconfirmation. It ranked near the top in the JevBench 2B-class evaluation, with its code, weights, and training data fully open-sourced, enabling execution on local hardware environments. Penguin Mail - Open-Source Rust Email Client for Linux Powered by AI Penguin Mail is an open-source Rust email client for Linux that supports multi-account unified management and local AI assistant features via LM Studio or Ollama. Enables unified management of email, calendars, and contacts across multiple accounts such as Gmail, Microsoft, and IMAP/POP3 within a single desktop client. Provides an optional AI assistant running in a local environment through LM Studio and Ollama integration, requiring user approval before executing key actions. Connects directly to account services without proprietary servers, tracking, or advertisements, and is released as open source under the GPL-3.0-or-later license. Show GN: Bringing Email Phishing Drills to Phone Calls — Unannounced Calls Prompted by a family member's experience as a victim, a mobile app was developed and released to provide unannounced, realistic voice phishing simulation drills. Enhanced realism by conducting drills via actual phone calls or text messages at random times during weekday daytime hours without prior notice. Responds using LLM classification based on real report transcripts, offering six types of phishing scenarios including parcel delivery and institutional impersonation. Released on both major app markets as a free personal app for public interest, primarily targeting middle-aged and older adults. Claude Code's Cleverest Feature Isn't for Users Speculation that Claude Code's next message suggestion feature may be a feedback collection mechanism for model training beyond a simple convenience feature, along with clarification from the developer. Claude Code's next message suggestion feature can be interpreted as a mechanism to collect high-quality RLHF preference data and learn task sequences through user edit history. Analysis suggests that edit data generated during actual development workflows is far more useful compared to conventional thumbs up/down ratings or using external evaluators. Anthropic developer edwinarbus clarified that the feature is not intended for collecting preference data, but was developed to help maintain workflows and assist with resuming sessions. Looking for developer-friendly inference providers who give you enough API credits to experiment [D] A post where a solo developer hitting API rate limits during multi-agent experiments asks for recommendations on developer-friendly inference providers that offer generous experimentation credits and rate limits. The author is building a repository indexing and benchmark generation tool that runs multiple agents in parallel using models such as Llama 3.3 70B and Qwen 2.5. While Together AI was initially sufficient, scaling up agents quickly led to hitting Requests Per Minute (RPM) and Tokens Per Minute (TPM) limits. The author expresses frustration that despite being satisfied with model quality, being a solo developer makes it difficult to immediately upgrade to enterprise paid tiers, preventing meaningful testing. NeurIPS 26 Event Metadata Deadline [D] Inquiry regarding the event metadata submission deadline and paper track selection issues for the NeurIPS conference Inquiry into whether the deadline for entering NeurIPS event metadata coincides with the camera-ready paper submission deadline Issues raised regarding all topic settings appearing as 'None' in the metadata fields Sharing of an issue where the AI4SocialScience (Economics and Finance) track selected during paper submission disappeared from the list Where to get started if you want to publish papers in Neurips,ACL,ICLR/A* conferences [R] This is an inquiry regarding the roadmap and preparatory steps for publishing papers in top-tier AI conferences such as NeurIPS, ACL, and ICLR, as well as advancing to PhD programs at top-tier schools. A second-year AI engineer is asking for a step-by-step research approach to start from the basics and publish papers in major A* conferences. The individual aims to pursue a Master's degree in Computer Science in the US in Fall 2028, followed by a PhD program at prestigious universities such as Stanford or Princeton. They are seeking advice on how to start research while working full-time and on key factors for being recognized as a promising researcher during PhD admissions. Best Books/Courses/Channels to Leapfrog on AI/ML Material A question asking for recommended learning resources, such as books, courses, and YouTube channels, to quickly catch up on the latest AI/ML developments since 2018–2019. A developer who took a hiatus after 2018–19 inquired about recommended learning materials (books, MOOCs, YouTube channels) to understand the latest AI/ML technology trends. The user is looking for roadmap-style resources, similar to TeachYourselfCS, that systematically guide through domain-wide knowledge and historical development. The user asked whether different approaches are needed to understand traditional ML models deployed at scale before 2021–2022 versus modern LLMs. Mistral says \"Le Chonk\" can challenge the best AI models French AI company Mistral has unveiled Mistral Large 4 (nicknamed 'Le Chonk'), a freely available 1-trillion-parameter model capable of competing with the top-performing models from the US and China. Mistral has released a preview version of Mistral Large 4 (Le Chonk), a 1-trillion-parameter model that anyone can use and fine-tune, with the official full release scheduled for later this month. While designed to compete with general-purpose models, it is optimized for specialized industrial sectors such as coding, cyber defense, manufacturing, finance, and electrical engineering. The company is pursuing a strategy to maximize the model's practical value by targeting various niche areas overlooked by other research labs. Google rolls out improved SynthID AI content detector, now available globally Google has globally launched a dedicated SynthID detection website featuring improved capabilities to detect watermarks from partner companies as well. Google launched a new dedicated website (SynthID.com) enabling anyone to directly check for AI-generated watermarks without going through the Gemini interface. Previously, it could only detect Google's proprietary watermarks, but the upgraded detector now supports SynthID watermarks from major partners, including ChatGPT-generated images. SynthID is a technology that embeds imperceptible signals into the pixels of AI-generated images and videos as well as audio waveforms, and has been applied to over 180 billion images and videos and 240,000 years of audio through Gemini alone. HiPHI: A Large-Scale Benchmark for High-Precision Human Motion and Object Interaction Introduces HiPHI, a large-scale motion capture benchmark dataset covering high-precision human motion and object interactions to bridge the data gap in humanoid robot learning. Presents the necessity of high-precision data that surpasses the limitations of existing internet videos and motion capture datasets for training humanoid robots, a key challenge in Embodied AI and Physical AI. Systematically collects extensive full-body motions utilizing FrameNet, a linguistic framework of human actions, and supports learning interactions such as carrying, pushing, and pulling by incorporating synchronized object trajectories and meshes. Demonstrates that reinforcement learning policies trained on this motion capture data improve with data scale and enable successful simulation-to-real (Sim-to-Real) transfer. AI in Production: What Breaks, What Works, and Who Approves It? | InfoQ Webinar This webinar covers engineering challenges, security risks, and governance issues that arise when deploying and operating AI agents and RAG pipelines in production environments. As AI agents expand into CI environments and internal systems, permission management, output validation, and accountability in the event of failures are emerging as key challenges. It addresses how to navigate tensions between platform engineers prioritizing development speed and autonomy, and security engineers aiming to establish data exposure limits and security boundaries. It shares real-world production use cases, including the scope of agent autonomy, pre-production verification frameworks, sensitive data exposure prevention, and production RAG operational strategies. Article: Building a Session-Ordered Kafka Pipeline in Go Presents a case study on building a Kafka pipeline in Go to achieve parallel processing while strictly ensuring intra-session message ordering in a large-scale conversational AI platform. Because Kafka partition ordering guarantees alone make it difficult to preserve order across multiple sessions, application-level routing, consistent hashing, and a per-session worker architecture were applied. Failed messages are retried in-place and a contiguous watermark commit mechanism is used to prevent message overtaking and ensure fault tolerance. To solve the blocking issue of a single worker pool, a two-tier worker hierarchy was introduced to achieve independent parallel processing and intra-session sequential processing simultaneously. Cloudflare Uses an AI Harness to Probe and Harden Its WAF Covers a case study where Cloudflare deployed state-of-the-art AI models within a controlled testing framework to probe vulnerabilities in its Web Application Firewall (WAF) and harden detection rules. Conducted black-box testing using attack payloads previously blocked by the WAF as starting points, guiding the AI model to vary encoding and delivery techniques based on feedback from prior responses. Across 45 scenarios, 1,107 mutation attempts yielded 49 valid findings, 48 of which were related to command injection or Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF). Based on the test results, two new detection rules (SSRF - Obfuscated Host, SSRF - Restricted Protocol) were added to Cloudflare's Managed Ruleset, and the existing SSRF - Cloud rule was improved. FBI Warns FortiBleed Remains Active After Amassing 86,644 Fortinet Device Credentials The U.S. FBI and Secret Service have warned that the 'FortiBleed' credential-harvesting campaign, which has compromised credentials for over 86,000 Fortinet devices, remains actively ongoing. A Russian-speaking threat actor obtained over 86,644 Fortinet device credentials across 194 countries worldwide by exploiting reused or leaked credentials and legacy SHA-256 storage methods. The campaign features a five-stage attack framework consisting of reconnaissance, credential stuffing/spraying, traffic interception via a Go-based sniffing tool (FortigateSniffer), GPU-accelerated cracking, and lateral movement leading to data exfiltration. The stolen credentials are refined through honeypot filtering and organizational valuation, and are presumed to be the work of an Initial Access Broker (IAB) affiliated with INC and Lynx ransomware operation groups. Anthropic Expands Claude Access for Vetted Cyber Teams as Glasswing Finds 129,000 Flaws Anthropic announced the discovery of large-scale software vulnerabilities through Project Glasswing and unveiled a program expanding access to Claude models with relaxed safety filters for vetted cybersecurity professionals. Anthropic's Project Glasswing discovered more than 129,000 verified vulnerabilities between April and July 2026, with over 33,000 classified as critical or high severity. The Cyber Verification Program (CVP) has been restructured into three tiers—Defensive, Red Team, and Special Access—providing vetted organizations with access to advanced models such as Claude Opus 5.5. According to VulnCheck analysis, the rate of discovered vulnerabilities exploited in actual attacks was approximately 0.67% (2 out of 300), confirming that even with a surge in AI-driven vulnerability detection, the likelihood of actual threats must be evaluated selectively. 100+ Compromised Websites Use Fake Cloudflare Checks to Deliver LunexStealer Over 100 compromised websites are distributing the info-stealing malware LunexStealer by prompting users to execute malicious commands via fake Cloudflare verification pages. The threat group UAC-0277 used the ClickFix technique, displaying forged Cloudflare security check screens to trick visitors into executing commands themselves. It leveraged the EtherHiding technique to retrieve malicious domains and operating modes from smart contracts on the Polygon or Ethereum blockchains. Distributed via malicious MSI packages, LunexStealer steals credentials, cookies, and files by additionally installing the browser extension LUNARAXE and the auxiliary module NAIVEMESS for file system control. Google unveils 'Nano Banana 2.1'... boosting performance and cutting costs in half Google has unveiled 'Nano Banana 2.1,' an image generation and editing model that enhances high-resolution generation quality and consistency maintenance while cutting image output costs by half. Developed based on Gemini 3.6 Flash, it enhances visual realism at 1K to 4K resolutions and improves tiling artifacts and editing consistency. Supporting an input context of up to 131,072 tokens and 14 reference images, it allows editing while maintaining the characteristics of up to 4 characters and 10 subjects. The image output cost has been halved compared to before, down to $30 per 1 million tokens, and is available via the Gemini API, AI Studio, Vertex AI, and more. \"AI makes phone calls on your behalf\"… Google Gemini spotted testing personal call-handling feature Signs have been spotted that Google is expanding Gemini's phone call delegation feature beyond existing business reservations and inquiries to simple personal calls with family and acquaintances. An analysis of the latest Google Gemini app update revealed screens related to 'Gemini Calling', which relays simple messages to friends or family or asks about their schedule. Rather than open-ended conversations, it is likely to be a limited call delegation service functioning like 'voice-transmitted text messages', such as giving notice of running late or checking RSVP status. To prevent unwanted AI calls, an introductory announcement disclosing that an AI is handling the call, along with detailed user settings for call reception and permission controls, is being prepared. Google Unveils On-Device ‘EmbeddingGemma 2’… Unified Processing of Text, Image, and Audio Google has unveiled 'EmbeddingGemma 2', a lightweight on-device multimodal embedding model capable of unified processing across text, code, images, video, and audio directly within devices such as smartphones and PCs. EmbeddingGemma 2 is a 740 million (740M) parameter modular model based on the Gemma 4 architecture, released under the Apache 2.0 license for commercial use. It supports a context window of up to 8,192 tokens and applies Matryoshka Representation Learning (MRL) to reduce local vector DB memory usage by up to 6 times. When running the full multimodal model on a Pixel 11 Pro with quantization, it consumes only about 567MB of RAM and supports major tools including MediaPipe, LiteRT, Ollama, and llama.cpp. Stuart Russell's Warning: \"AI Alignment Is Impossible... The Limitations of Imitation Learning and RLHF\" AI scholar Professor Stuart Russell warned that current LLM alignment approaches based on imitation learning and RLHF are structurally doomed to fail, proposing the 'Assistance Games' model as an alternative. Pre-training that mimics internet data and RLHF methods that aim for short-term rewards induce deceptive and sycophantic behavior in AI, making flawless alignment structurally impossible. Instead of assuming that human objectives are fully known, an 'Assistance Games' model is needed to ensure AI maintains uncertainty about human preferences. If safety cannot be mathematically and scientifically proven, AI development must be halted, as misaligned autonomous goal-seeking could lead to catastrophic socio-economic disasters. Anthropic Integrates with Google Workspace... Call 'Claude' Directly Within Documents Anthropic has launched 'Claude for Google Workspace' integration in public beta, allowing users to directly invoke Claude to work within Google Docs, Sheets, and Slides. Through the Google Workspace add-on, users can directly run Claude from the in-document sidebar to edit content, write formulas, and generate slides. Users can directly control Claude's editing autonomy through the 'Ask before making changes' and 'Allow all edits' options. Security controls for Enterprise plans and dedicated Workspace connectors that can combine with other productivity tools like Salesforce are also supported. Google Officially Launches Image Model 'Nano Banana 2.1'... Improved Text Rendering Google has officially launched 'Gemini Nano Banana 2.1', an efficiency-focused image generation model that improves image quality, text rendering, and character consistency, while resolving tiling seam artifacts. Google has released 'Gemini Nano Banana 2.1', which enhances text rendering, image quality, and character consistency during multi-turn editing while maintaining Flash-tier speed and cost. It supports up to 14 reference image inputs, resolves tiling seam artifacts that occurred when generating images with extreme aspect ratios at 2K and 4K resolutions, and supports search grounding. The existing 'Gemini 3.1 Flash Image' model will be sunset on October 29, and developers have been advised to migrate to the new model. MongoDB: \"Transitioning to Production AI Services Hinges on Data Connectivity and Operational History Management\" MongoDB emphasized the importance of data modernization, contextual connectivity, and managing agent operational history for enterprises to successfully transition to production AI services. To overcome limitations in enterprise AI adoption, data modernization based on a document data model and contextual connectivity across data are essential. Search accuracy in AI applications must be improved by combining vector search, keyword search, metadata filtering, and reranking. Storing agent inputs, actions, and results in a database to manage their history enables traceability and context sharing across multi-agent systems. Creating Enterprise AI via Conversation... Wisenut Accelerates Popularization of AI Agents Wisenut has unveiled 'Wise Agent Labs', a tool that allows users to create and manage enterprise AI agents purely through conversation without coding, expanding into public and private sectors. At 'AI Festa 26', Wisenut unveiled 'Wise Agent Labs', which enables creating and modifying enterprise AI agents through conversation without specialized coding. The solution supports multi-LLM, RAG, MCP integration, and real-time monitoring, and recently achieved GS Certification Grade 1. With more than 80 deployment cases across finance, defense, and other sectors, Wisenut recorded a 71.5% market share in the AI agent sector of the Public Procurement Service Digital Service Mall in the first half of this year. Preventing AI Jailbreaking and Data Leaks: ITCEN CLOIT Unveils Enterprise 'Guardrails' ITCEN CLOIT has unveiled 'AgentGo Guard,' an enterprise AI security solution that applies bidirectional security and governance to queries and responses without replacing existing AI infrastructure. Designed with an architecture that adds security features without replacing existing internal chatbots, RAG, or on-premises LLMs Applies bidirectional security, including blocking prompt injection and jailbreaking at the input stage, and scanning for personal data exposure and harmful content at the output stage Supports diverse deployment environments including public cloud, on-premises, and air-gapped networks, while providing centralized security policy and usage monitoring via a dashboard \"After GPUs Comes the Data Bottleneck\"… Dinoticia Reshapes AI Infrastructure with VDPU Dinoticia presented a Vector Database Processing Unit (VDPU)-based AI storage infrastructure to resolve data retrieval and memory bottlenecks in the era of AI agents. With the advancement of AI agents driving a surge in data retrieval volume and KV cache, data center bottlenecks are shifting from GPUs to latency in CPU and memory data movement. Dinoticia developed the VDPU, a dedicated search semiconductor that boosts retrieval speeds by up to 15 times compared to conventional CPUs and reduces latency using zero-copy technology. The company aims to build an AI storage system that rapidly feeds data to GPUs by combining VDPU hardware with comprehensive memory management software."